---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — GPS-Go';
const description = 'Privacy policy for GPS-Go (com.yanerapps.gpsgo), a no-root Android location simulator by Yaner Apps.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} currentPath="/privacy/">
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="prose-header">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">GPS-Go for Android (<code>com.yanerapps.gpsgo</code>)</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Effective: 31 Aug 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated: 21 Sep 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <a class="lang-switch" href="/privacy/zh/">中文版本</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This policy explains what GPS-Go does with your data. GPS-Go is developed by Yaner Apps
      ("we", "us"). It applies to the GPS-Go Android application and to this website.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> GPS-Go has no user accounts and no servers of our own. Your simulated
      locations, history, saved places, tags, and routes stay on your device. Three third parties receive
      data when you use specific features: Google Maps and Google Places (map display and place search),
      Geoapify (place search), and Google AdMob (advertising). We never sell your personal information.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Data that stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The following is stored locally in the app's private storage and is never transmitted to us &mdash; we
      operate no server and have no way to read it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simulated coordinates, custom names, favourites, and location history</li>
      <li>Tags, saved routes, and records of routes you have run</li>
      <li>Your local search history</li>
      <li>App settings and preferences (units, theme, joystick style, and so on)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can delete this data inside the app at any time. Uninstalling GPS-Go removes all of it.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Your device's real location</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go requests location permission so it can show where you actually are on the map (the "Locate"
      button) and so it can register itself as a mock location provider. Your real location is used on the
      device only. We do not transmit it, store it on any server, or share it with anyone. Coordinates may
      be sent to Google or Geoapify only as part of a map or search request you initiate &mdash; see section 3.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Data sent to third parties</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go transmits data off your device only in the situations below. Each is tied to a feature you
      actively use.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Recipient</th>
            <th>What is sent</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Maps Platform</td>
            <td>Map viewport coordinates and zoom level</td>
            <td>To render the map</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Places</td>
            <td>Your search text and, for biasing results, an approximate location</td>
            <td>To return place suggestions and their coordinates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Geoapify (OpenStreetMap data)</td>
            <td>Your search text and, for biasing results, an approximate location</td>
            <td>To return place suggestions and their coordinates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google AdMob and the Google User Messaging Platform</td>
            <td>Your advertising ID (AAID), device and app information sent with an ad request, and your consent choices</td>
            <td>To request and display rewarded video, interstitial, and banner ads, and to record your consent</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Place searches are routed to Geoapify where possible and fall back to Google Places. Both providers
      receive only the text you type and, at most, an approximate location &mdash; never your location history.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a search result comes from Google Places, its coordinates are removed from your local search
      history 30 days after the search, as required by Google's terms. Results from Geoapify and
      coordinates you enter yourself are kept until you delete them.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go displays ads supplied by Google AdMob, including rewarded video ads, interstitial ads, and
      banner ads:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rewarded video ads:</strong> Never forced on you; only play after you tap a button offering extra daily usage in exchange for watching an ad.</li>
      <li><strong>Interstitial ads:</strong> May appear between activity transitions, subject to daily frequency caps and cooldown intervals.</li>
      <li><strong>Banner ads:</strong> Displayed inline within certain app screens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No ads are ever shown in the floating joystick overlay or in the ongoing notification.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use Google AdMob only. We do not use any other ad network, mediation layer, or analytics or
      crash-reporting SDK. We never send your location history, saved places, or routes to any advertising
      service, and we never attach location data to an ad request as a custom parameter.
    </p>

    <h3>Consent (EEA, UK, and Switzerland)</h3>
    <p>
      If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, or Switzerland, GPS-Go asks for your
      consent before ads are requested, using Google's certified User Messaging Platform. You can review or
      change your choice at any time in the app under <strong>Settings &rarr; About &rarr; Privacy Options</strong>.
      If you decline, the app remains fully usable and the ad-gated actions are granted without an ad.
    </p>

    <h3>US state privacy laws</h3>
    <p>
      We do not sell your personal information, and we do not share it for cross-context behavioural
      advertising in exchange for money. Residents of US states with applicable privacy laws are shown a
      notice with an opt-out choice, delivered through the same Privacy Options screen.
    </p>

    <h3>Controlling the advertising ID</h3>
    <p>
      Android lets you reset or delete your advertising ID at any time under
      <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Ads</strong> (the exact path varies by device). Deleting it stops
      personalised advertising system-wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google's handling of ad data is governed by the
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Privacy Policy</a>; see also
      <a href="https://support.google.com/admob/answer/6128543" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how Google uses data in AdMob</a>.
      Geoapify's handling is governed by the
      <a href="https://www.geoapify.com/privacy-policy/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Geoapify Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Backup export files</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go can export your data as a file to a location you choose. That file contains your location
      history, saved places, tags, and routes in plain form. It is created only when you ask for it, it is
      not uploaded anywhere, and once it leaves the app it is yours to protect. Be careful where you store
      or send it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android's own cloud backup may include app data if you have enabled it on your device. That backup is
      managed by Google under your Google account, not by us.
    </p>

    <h2>6. What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No user accounts, sign-in, or profiles</li>
      <li>No servers operated by us, and therefore no copy of your data held by us</li>
      <li>No analytics or crash-reporting SDKs</li>
      <li>No sale of personal information</li>
      <li>No tracking of other people &mdash; GPS-Go simulates only your own device's location</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Children</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go is intended for adults. It is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect data
      from children. Its Google Play target audience is set to adults only.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Because your data lives on your device, you control its lifetime. The app deletes history older than
      the retention window you configure, and every record can be deleted individually or in bulk from
      within the app. Uninstalling removes everything. There is nothing for us to delete on our side, since
      we hold nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Data held by Google or Geoapify as a result of the requests described in section 3 is subject to
      their own retention practices and their own policies, linked above.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Security</h2>
    <p>
      All network requests made by the app use HTTPS. Local data is kept in the app's private storage,
      which other apps cannot read on a non-rooted device.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, delete, or port your personal
      data, or to object to certain processing. Since GPS-Go stores your data only on your device, you can
      exercise these rights directly in the app. For anything related to advertising data held by Google,
      use the Privacy Options screen in the app and Google's own controls. If you need help, write to
      <a href="mailto:yanerapps@gmail.com">yanerapps@gmail.com</a> and we will respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new "last updated" date.
      Material changes affecting how data is shared will also be noted in the app's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Yaner Apps &mdash; <a href="mailto:yanerapps@gmail.com">yanerapps@gmail.com</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
